From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: do not disarm the watchdog before claiming the IRQ

cedrus_irq() cancels the decode watchdog as its first act, and only then
works out whether the interrupt was even ours. If it was not -- a spurious
interrupt, or a status register that reads zero -- it returns IRQ_NONE with
the job still marked TRANS_RUNNING and nothing left alive to finish it.

That job never completes. The decode hangs, and the next VIDIOC_STREAMOFF
sleeps forever in v4l2_m2m_cancel_job() waiting for TRANS_RUNNING to clear,
in uninterruptible sleep where it does not take SIGKILL. From there the
device is unusable for every client until reboot. cedrus provides no
job_abort op -- the hook v4l2_m2m_cancel_job() calls before it starts
waiting -- so the watchdog is the ONLY thing that can end a job that the
hardware will not finish, and it must stay armed on every path that does not
finish the job here.

Reorder so the watchdog is disarmed only once the interrupt is known to be
ours, and handle the two remaining paths deliberately:

  * No current context: cancel the watchdog and return. There is no running
    job to orphan, and leaving a stale timer pending is actively harmful --
    schedule_delayed_work() will not move an already-pending expiry, so the
    NEXT job would inherit this one's deadline and could be declared timed
    out early.

  * Watchdog already ran: acknowledge the engine before returning, rather
    than returning immediately. This line is level-triggered on some boards
    (IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH on the H713), so a status bit left set with nothing
    to clear it is re-raised for as long as the device stays powered.

MEASURED, not argued. With patch 0060's injector forcing the "not ours"
branch on demand:

  before   the decode never returns; ffmpeg ends in D state inside
           v4l2_m2m_cancel_job(), unkillable, and the VE is dead for every
           later client until reboot
  after    the watchdog fires 2 s later, ends the job with VB2_BUF_STATE_ERROR,
           ffmpeg exits with an error, and the next decode is bit-exact

The upstream ordering has the same hole on every SoC cedrus supports; it is
simply hard to reach without a spurious interrupt to trigger it.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
@@ -135,24 +135,49 @@
 	enum vb2_buffer_state state;
 	enum cedrus_irq_status status;
 
-	/*
-	 * If cancel_delayed_work returns false it means watchdog already
-	 * executed and finished the job.
-	 */
-	if (!cancel_delayed_work(&dev->watchdog_work))
-		return IRQ_HANDLED;
-
 	ctx = v4l2_m2m_get_curr_priv(dev->m2m_dev);
 	if (!ctx) {
+		/*
+		 * No job is running, so any pending watchdog is stale and
+		 * cancelling it orphans nothing. Leaving it armed would be
+		 * worse than useless: schedule_delayed_work() does not move an
+		 * already-pending timer, so the NEXT job would inherit this
+		 * one's expiry and could be declared timed out early.
+		 */
+		cancel_delayed_work(&dev->watchdog_work);
 		v4l2_err(&dev->v4l2_dev,
 			 "Instance released before the end of transaction\n");
 		return IRQ_NONE;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Decide whether this interrupt is ours BEFORE disarming the watchdog.
+	 * The original order cancelled it first and then took this early
+	 * return, which left the job with TRANS_RUNNING set and nothing left
+	 * to finish it: the decode never completes, and the next
+	 * VIDIOC_STREAMOFF sleeps forever in v4l2_m2m_cancel_job() in D state,
+	 * where it does not even take SIGKILL. cedrus offers no job_abort op,
+	 * so the watchdog is the only thing that can end such a job -- it must
+	 * stay armed on every path that does not finish the job here.
+	 */
 	status = ctx->current_codec->irq_status(ctx);
 	if (status == CEDRUS_IRQ_NONE)
 		return IRQ_NONE;
 
+	/*
+	 * If cancel_delayed_work returns false the watchdog already ran and
+	 * finished the job. Acknowledge the engine anyway rather than
+	 * returning immediately: this interrupt line is level-triggered on
+	 * some boards (IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH on the H713), and a status bit left
+	 * set with nothing to clear it is re-raised for as long as the device
+	 * stays powered.
+	 */
+	if (!cancel_delayed_work(&dev->watchdog_work)) {
+		ctx->current_codec->irq_disable(ctx);
+		ctx->current_codec->irq_clear(ctx);
+		return IRQ_HANDLED;
+	}
+
 	ctx->current_codec->irq_disable(ctx);
 	ctx->current_codec->irq_clear(ctx);
 
